-- title:
--   Non-vanishing of a local GL₃× GL₂ Rankin–Selberg integral
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a finite place of $\mathbb{Q}$ (a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$) and write $\psi =$ `psiLocal` $\mathbb{Q}\,p$ for the standard local additive character of the completion $\mathbb{Q}_p$. Let $W_3^{\mathrm{base}} : GL_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)\to\mathbb{C}$ satisfy: the Whittaker transformation law $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}(u(x,y,z)g) = \psi^{-1}(x+y)\,W_3^{\mathrm{base}}(g)$ for all $x,y,z$ and $g$, where $u(x,y,z)$ is the upper triangular unipotent matrix with entries $x,y,z$; right invariance under some open subgroup of $GL_3(\mathbb{Q}_p)$; $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}\neq 0$; cyclicity in both directions, namely every non-zero $W$ in the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the right translates of $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}$ has $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}$ in the span of its own right translates; and finiteness of invariants, namely for every open subgroup $U_v$ there is a finite set $B$ of functions spanning all members of that span which are right $U_v$-invariant. Let $w : GL_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)\to\mathbb{C}$ satisfy $w(u(x)g)=\psi(x)w(g)$ for the standard unipotent $u(x)$, be right invariant under some open subgroup, and be non-zero. Then, with $GL_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ carrying its Borel structure, for every Haar measure $\mu_2$ on $GL_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ and every Haar measure $\mu_{N_2}$ on the image $N_2$ of `unipotentGL2Hom`, there are $W_3$ in the span of the right translates of $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}$ and $\sigma\in\mathbb{R}$ such that for all $s$ with $\operatorname{Re} s > \sigma$ the integral $\int (W_3(\iota g)\,w(g))\,\lVert\det g\rVert^{s-1/2}$, taken against $\mu_2$ weighted by the quotient density of $N_2$ relative to $\mu_{N_2}$, is non-zero; here $\iota$ is the block embedding $h\mapsto \operatorname{diag}(h,1)$ of $GL_2$ into $GL_3$ and the modulus is that of `modulus`.
--
--   This is the one-sided non-degeneracy of the local $GL_3\times GL_2$ Rankin–Selberg pairing of Jacquet, Piatetski-Shapiro and Shalika: a fixed non-zero $\psi$-Whittaker function on $GL_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ pairs non-trivially, in a right half-plane, with some vector of the Whittaker model generated by $W_3^{\mathrm{base}}$. It feeds the local input of the converse-theorem argument used in the cubic-induction step of the Langlands–Tunnell construction.
